1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,488
In October 1707, a fleet of British warships was sailing home from the

2
00:00:05,538 --> 00:00:10,616
Mediterranean, feeling its way through fog and heavy seas toward the English

3
00:00:10,666 --> 00:00:11,525
Channel.

4
00:00:12,025 --> 00:00:14,684
For days, cloud had hidden the Sun and stars,

5
00:00:14,734 --> 00:00:17,338
so the navigators couldn't take a sighting.

6
00:00:17,388 --> 00:00:19,342
Their position was an estimate.

7
00:00:19,392 --> 00:00:20,913
The estimate was wrong.

8
00:00:21,713 --> 00:00:26,431
On the night of the twenty second, the flagship Association struck the rocks of the

9
00:00:26,481 --> 00:00:29,148
Isles of Scilly, off the tip of Cornwall.

10
00:00:29,198 --> 00:00:31,144
Three more ships followed her.

11
00:00:31,194 --> 00:00:35,080
Somewhere between fourteen hundred and two thousand sailors drowned,

12
00:00:35,130 --> 00:00:38,130
among them the admiral, Sir Cloudesley Shovell.

13
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:42,305
Historians still argue about exactly what went wrong:

14
00:00:42,355 --> 00:00:45,485
bad charts, currents, errors in reckoning.

15
00:00:45,535 --> 00:00:50,562
But the disaster became a symbol of a problem every sailor of the age shared.

16
00:00:50,612 --> 00:00:54,537
Even on a clear day, with the best instruments in the world,

17
00:00:54,587 --> 00:00:58,268
no one at sea could say how far east or west they were.

18
00:00:59,068 --> 00:01:03,107
The answer, when it finally came, didn't come from an astronomer.

19
00:01:03,157 --> 00:01:06,487
It came from a village carpenter from the north of England,

20
00:01:06,537 --> 00:01:07,468
and a clock.

21
00:01:08,818 --> 00:01:11,085
A position at sea is two numbers.

22
00:01:11,135 --> 00:01:14,767
The first, latitude, is how far north or south you are,

23
00:01:14,817 --> 00:01:16,431
and it's the easy one.

24
00:01:16,481 --> 00:01:20,232
At noon, measure how high the Sun stands above the horizon,

25
00:01:20,282 --> 00:01:22,667
look up a table, and you have it.

26
00:01:22,717 --> 00:01:26,469
At night, the height of the Pole Star does the same job.

27
00:01:27,269 --> 00:01:29,896
Longitude, how far east or west you are,

28
00:01:29,946 --> 00:01:32,096
is a different kind of problem.

29
00:01:32,146 --> 00:01:36,914
The Earth turns under the sky, so every line of longitude sees the same sky,

30
00:01:36,964 --> 00:01:38,758
just at a different time.

31
00:01:38,808 --> 00:01:42,327
There's nothing overhead that tells you where you are.

32
00:01:43,677 --> 00:01:45,514
But that's also the way in.

33
00:01:45,564 --> 00:01:49,973
The Earth turns three hundred and sixty degrees every twenty four hours.

34
00:01:50,023 --> 00:01:52,260
That's fifteen degrees every hour.

35
00:01:52,310 --> 00:01:56,034
So a difference in longitude is really a difference in time.

36
00:01:56,834 --> 00:02:00,668
Suppose you carry a clock set to the time at your home port.

37
00:02:00,718 --> 00:02:04,258
Out at sea, you watch for the moment the Sun is highest:

38
00:02:04,308 --> 00:02:05,259
local noon.

39
00:02:05,309 --> 00:02:09,673
If your clock says it's three in the afternoon back home at that moment,

40
00:02:09,723 --> 00:02:11,851
you're three hours behind home.

41
00:02:11,901 --> 00:02:13,616
Forty five degrees west.

42
00:02:14,416 --> 00:02:18,850
In 1714, Parliament offered a prize of twenty thousand pounds,

43
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:24,604
a fortune at the time, for any practical method of finding longitude to within half

44
00:02:24,654 --> 00:02:28,352
a degree, proven on a voyage to the West Indies.

45
00:02:29,152 --> 00:02:32,245
Half a degree of longitude is two minutes of time.

46
00:02:32,295 --> 00:02:35,331
A voyage to the West Indies took about six weeks.

47
00:02:35,381 --> 00:02:39,259
So a clock would have to stay within two minutes over six weeks.

48
00:02:39,309 --> 00:02:42,458
Less than three seconds a day, on a rolling ship.

49
00:02:43,258 --> 00:02:45,790
Strictly, it didn't have to be perfect,

50
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:47,139
only predictable.

51
00:02:47,189 --> 00:02:50,836
A clock that always gains exactly ten seconds a day is fine,

52
00:02:50,886 --> 00:02:52,655
because you can subtract.

53
00:02:52,705 --> 00:02:54,827
What it couldn't do was wander.

54
00:02:55,627 --> 00:02:59,171
And the best clocks of the day kept time with a pendulum,

55
00:02:59,221 --> 00:03:01,028
which is hopeless at sea.

56
00:03:01,078 --> 00:03:04,622
Every roll of the deck adds its own push to the swing.

57
00:03:04,672 --> 00:03:07,198
Heat makes a pendulum longer and slower,

58
00:03:07,248 --> 00:03:11,750
and on the way to the Caribbean, the temperature changes enormously.

59
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:16,669
Most experts assumed the answer would come from the sky instead.

60
00:03:16,719 --> 00:03:20,839
The Moon moves against the stars by about its own width every hour,

61
00:03:20,889 --> 00:03:23,876
so in principle it's a clock anyone can read.

62
00:03:23,926 --> 00:03:27,212
Measure the angle between the Moon and a bright star,

63
00:03:27,262 --> 00:03:31,441
compare it with predicted tables, and you get the time at home.

64
00:03:31,941 --> 00:03:36,547
The trouble was that the Moon's motion is fiendishly complicated.

65
00:03:36,597 --> 00:03:39,891
Good enough tables didn't exist until the 1750s,

66
00:03:39,941 --> 00:03:44,744
and turning one measurement into a position took a long calculation.

67
00:03:46,094 --> 00:03:49,132
John Harrison was born in Yorkshire in 1693,

68
00:03:49,182 --> 00:03:53,008
the son of a carpenter, and trained as one himself.

69
00:03:53,058 --> 00:03:58,461
He taught himself clockmaking, and his early clocks were made almost entirely of

70
00:03:58,511 --> 00:03:59,183
wood.

71
00:03:59,983 --> 00:04:03,257
He cut the wheels from oak and used lignum vitae,

72
00:04:03,307 --> 00:04:06,901
a tropical hardwood so oily that it lubricates itself,

73
00:04:06,951 --> 00:04:08,755
for the parts that rub.

74
00:04:08,805 --> 00:04:12,207
Clock oil in his day went gummy and stopped clocks,

75
00:04:12,257 --> 00:04:15,148
so Harrison simply designed the oil out.

76
00:04:15,948 --> 00:04:17,928
Then he tackled temperature.

77
00:04:17,978 --> 00:04:21,331
His gridiron pendulum uses rods of steel and brass.

78
00:04:21,381 --> 00:04:24,137
Brass expands more than steel when it warms,

79
00:04:24,187 --> 00:04:29,093
and the rods are arranged so the steel lets the weight down while the brass pushes

80
00:04:29,143 --> 00:04:30,108
it back up.

81
00:04:30,158 --> 00:04:32,735
With the right lengths, the two cancel,

82
00:04:32,785 --> 00:04:35,422
and the pendulum stays the same length.

83
00:04:36,222 --> 00:04:41,108
By the mid-1720s, Harrison claimed his best clocks were keeping time to about a

84
00:04:41,158 --> 00:04:44,660
second a month, far better than anything in London.

85
00:04:44,710 --> 00:04:46,767
But they still had pendulums.

86
00:04:48,117 --> 00:04:53,128
In 1730 he went to London with a plan for a clock that could go to sea.

87
00:04:53,178 --> 00:04:56,677
The famous clockmaker George Graham lent him money,

88
00:04:56,727 --> 00:05:02,329
and five years later he had built H1: a brass machine with no pendulum at all.

89
00:05:03,129 --> 00:05:06,746
Instead, two dumbbell-shaped balances rock back and forth,

90
00:05:06,796 --> 00:05:10,006
linked so they always move in opposite directions.

91
00:05:10,056 --> 00:05:14,488
When the ship lurches, it pushes one balance forward and the other back,

92
00:05:14,538 --> 00:05:16,409
and the two effects cancel.

93
00:05:17,209 --> 00:05:20,492
In 1736 it went to sea, to Lisbon and back.

94
00:05:20,542 --> 00:05:23,499
As England came into sight on the way home,

95
00:05:23,549 --> 00:05:28,858
Harrison told the officers that the headland they took for Start Point was really

96
00:05:28,908 --> 00:05:32,191
the Lizard, about sixty miles further west.

97
00:05:32,241 --> 00:05:33,433
He was right.

98
00:05:34,233 --> 00:05:36,158
Harrison wasn't satisfied.

99
00:05:36,208 --> 00:05:39,738
He spent more than twenty years on two more sea clocks,

100
00:05:39,788 --> 00:05:43,257
and along the way invented two things we still use.

101
00:05:43,307 --> 00:05:46,590
The bimetallic strip: two metals bonded together,

102
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:49,368
which bends as the temperature changes.

103
00:05:49,418 --> 00:05:51,960
It's still inside thermostats today.

104
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:57,508
And the caged roller bearing, which lets a shaft turn on rollers held apart in a

105
00:05:57,558 --> 00:06:00,456
cage, so they don't rub against each other.

106
00:06:00,506 --> 00:06:05,080
You'll find its descendants in almost every machine with a spinning shaft.

107
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:09,189
But the third clock, H3, took him nineteen years,

108
00:06:09,239 --> 00:06:11,648
and it never kept time well enough.

109
00:06:11,698 --> 00:06:14,588
Large, slow balances were the wrong idea.

110
00:06:15,938 --> 00:06:18,836
His fourth timekeeper, finished in 1759,

111
00:06:18,886 --> 00:06:21,653
looks almost nothing like the others.

112
00:06:21,703 --> 00:06:25,388
H4 is a watch, about thirteen centimetres across.

113
00:06:25,438 --> 00:06:27,746
You could hold it in one hand.

114
00:06:28,546 --> 00:06:30,103
The secret is speed.

115
00:06:30,153 --> 00:06:32,700
Its balance swings five times a second,

116
00:06:32,750 --> 00:06:36,285
far faster than the slow balances of the sea clocks.

117
00:06:36,335 --> 00:06:39,005
A small, fast balance holds its rhythm,

118
00:06:39,055 --> 00:06:42,097
and the motion of a ship barely disturbs it.

119
00:06:42,897 --> 00:06:46,636
Each swing is kept going by tiny pallets cut from diamond,

120
00:06:46,686 --> 00:06:50,177
about one millimetre by two, so they hardly wear.

121
00:06:50,227 --> 00:06:55,332
And a bimetallic strip adjusts the balance spring as the temperature changes.

122
00:06:56,132 --> 00:06:58,928
To keep the drive steady, H4 uses a fusee,

123
00:06:58,978 --> 00:07:00,563
a cone-shaped pulley.

124
00:07:00,613 --> 00:07:03,712
When the mainspring is fully wound it pulls hard,

125
00:07:03,762 --> 00:07:07,043
so its chain works on the narrow end of the cone.

126
00:07:07,093 --> 00:07:11,040
As the spring runs down, the chain moves toward the wide end,

127
00:07:11,090 --> 00:07:13,099
where it gets more leverage.

128
00:07:13,149 --> 00:07:16,611
The push reaching the balance stays nearly constant.

129
00:07:17,961 --> 00:07:22,779
In November 1761, H4 sailed for Jamaica aboard HMS Deptford,

130
00:07:22,829 --> 00:07:26,299
in the care of Harrison's son, William.

131
00:07:26,349 --> 00:07:29,370
The crossing took about nine weeks.

132
00:07:30,170 --> 00:07:32,873
When they arrived, William worked out that,

133
00:07:32,923 --> 00:07:37,174
after allowing for its rate, the watch was just over five seconds out,

134
00:07:37,224 --> 00:07:40,213
eighty one days after it had last been checked.

135
00:07:40,263 --> 00:07:43,941
In longitude, that's an error of little more than a mile.

136
00:07:44,741 --> 00:07:47,790
The Board of Longitude, which ran the prize,

137
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:49,244
wasn't satisfied.

138
00:07:49,294 --> 00:07:52,786
Harrison hadn't declared the watch's rate in advance,

139
00:07:52,836 --> 00:07:56,138
and even Jamaica's own longitude was uncertain.

140
00:07:56,188 --> 00:07:59,490
So in 1764, with the rate stated before sailing,

141
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:01,134
H4 went to Barbados.

142
00:08:01,184 --> 00:08:05,055
Over forty seven days, its error was thirty nine seconds,

143
00:08:05,105 --> 00:08:06,446
about ten miles.

144
00:08:06,496 --> 00:08:09,545
Three times better than the prize demanded.

145
00:08:10,895 --> 00:08:12,627
It still wasn't enough.

146
00:08:12,677 --> 00:08:17,481
Several members of the Board were astronomers who favoured the lunar method,

147
00:08:17,531 --> 00:08:21,843
including Nevil Maskelyne, who became Astronomer Royal in 1765.

148
00:08:21,893 --> 00:08:24,792
One watch, they argued, might be luck.

149
00:08:24,842 --> 00:08:28,602
The question was whether anyone else could make another.

150
00:08:29,402 --> 00:08:32,556
So in 1765 Harrison was paid half the prize,

151
00:08:32,606 --> 00:08:38,077
ten thousand pounds, on condition that he took H4 apart in front of a panel of

152
00:08:38,127 --> 00:08:42,166
experts, explained every part, and handed it over.

153
00:08:42,216 --> 00:08:46,733
The Board then had the watchmaker Larcum Kendall make a copy.

154
00:08:47,533 --> 00:08:54,227
That copy, known as K1, sailed to the Pacific with Captain James Cook on his second

155
00:08:54,277 --> 00:08:56,832
voyage, from 1772 to 1775.

156
00:08:56,882 --> 00:09:01,660
Cook called it his trusty friend and never-failing guide.

157
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:06,012
Meanwhile Harrison, now in his late seventies,

158
00:09:06,062 --> 00:09:08,343
built one more watch, H5.

159
00:09:08,393 --> 00:09:13,357
It was tested at King George the Third's observatory at Kew in 1772,

160
00:09:13,407 --> 00:09:16,182
and the King took Harrison's side.

161
00:09:16,232 --> 00:09:21,974
In 1773, Parliament awarded Harrison a further eight thousand seven hundred and

162
00:09:22,024 --> 00:09:23,315
fifty pounds.

163
00:09:24,115 --> 00:09:28,647
Over the years he'd received more than twenty three thousand pounds,

164
00:09:28,697 --> 00:09:30,777
more than the prize itself.

165
00:09:30,827 --> 00:09:34,843
But the Board never formally declared that he had won it.

166
00:09:34,893 --> 00:09:36,264
He died in 1776.

167
00:09:37,614 --> 00:09:40,926
Within a few decades, other makers had found simpler,

168
00:09:40,976 --> 00:09:45,389
cheaper designs, and marine chronometers were becoming common at sea.

169
00:09:45,439 --> 00:09:47,939
The lunar method didn't vanish either.

170
00:09:47,989 --> 00:09:51,242
Navigators used both, and each checked the other.

171
00:09:52,042 --> 00:09:54,814
And the idea underneath never went away.

172
00:09:54,864 --> 00:09:58,924
Your phone finds its position by timing signals from satellites,

173
00:09:58,974 --> 00:10:01,378
each one carrying an atomic clock.

174
00:10:01,428 --> 00:10:06,469
Finding where you are is still a question of knowing exactly what time it is.

175
00:10:06,519 --> 00:10:08,862
That's a story for another video.
